WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:05.740
So there's this thing in your head called
a brain, and it seems to be where thoughts

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.720
and feelings and memories and perceptions
and everything elses happen, so it would be

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.710
great to know how it works. The obvious way
to see how something works is to pull it out,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.859
open it up and have a look, but when you do
that with a brain, it stops working, and tends

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.919
to upset its owner, so what we really need
are ways to study brains while they're working

00:00:23.919 --> 00:00:29.029
inside people's heads. Which... isn't easy,
but can be done with a range of different

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:35.359
brain imaging techniques.
One of the popular techniques is MEG, magnetoencephalography,

00:00:35.359 --> 00:00:40.960
which is much less scary than it sounds. Magneto
means magnetism, encephalo means brain, and

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.920
ography means images, so magnetoencephalography
means images of the magnetism of the brain.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.989
'Cause the brain is made up of billions of
cells called neurons. When the brain does

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:54.839
stuff, these neurons are sending electrical
signals to each other, which, like all electrical

00:00:54.839 --> 00:01:01.219
signals, generate magnetic fields. Magnetoencephalography
measures and locates these magnetic fields,

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:05.280
which indicate the electrical activity of
the neurons, which is the activity of the

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.640
brain. With magnetoencephalography, we can
see the brain working, as its working, inside

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.720
someone's head.
The magnetic fields caused by brain activity

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.530
are tiny and faint so we measure them with
highly sensitive magnetism detectors called

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.840
SQUIDs, just like you’d use a highly sensitive
microphone to measure a very quiet sound.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.070
If you were measuring a very quiet sound you’d
have to do in a very quiet room or else you

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.540
couldn’t hear the sound over the noise of
air conditioning next door and cars down the

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.729
road – and you get the same problem detecting
the brain’s magnetism over the magnetism

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:41.770
coming from nearby phones and computers and
from the earth itself. To block out this noise,

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:46.409
MEG is performed in a shielded room built
with thick layers of metal that block magnetic

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:51.380
fields from outside. Another complication
is that SQUIDs, our super-sensitive magnetism

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:57.130
detectors, only work when they’re cooled
down to about minus 270 degrees Celsius, which

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:02.570
is almost the absolute coldest it is possible
for anything to be. Liquid helium can be used

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.509
to keep the SQUIDs this cool. The SQUIDs are
then arranged in a sort of a helmet, with

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.160
a few hundred of the detectors held right
up close to the head. Some specialised systems

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.709
have smaller helmets to better fit children.
The SQUIDs do need to be close because they

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:20.480
can only detect brain activity a few centimetres
into the brain, and even then only some kinds

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.510
of brain activity.
But overall, MEG is still a pretty great brain imaging

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.190
technique, partly because the process is so
easy. The subject just sticks their head in

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.930
the helmet, lies very still, and a detailed
record is made of their brain activity. It’s

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:38.260
non-invasive, meaning we don’t have to cut
anyone open, or inject anything, or expose

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.790
anyone to radiation, which a lot of the other
techniques do involve. It also has really

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:47.239
good spatial and temporal resolution, meaning
it captures a lot of detail and can do so

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:53.349
thousands of times per second so we can see
changes in the brain moment-by-moment. Techniques

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:58.470
like EEG have less detail, and MRI can only
take an image every second or two. It’s

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.840
important to keep in mind, though, that MEG
doesn’t show the physical structure of the

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.049
brain – just its activity – so MEG scans
are often combined with MRI scans so we can

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:13.080
see both together. But yeah. MEG has a lot
of advantages as a brain imaging technique,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:17.049
and because of these advantages, it’s widely
used in research and diagnosis, where it can

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:20.730
show which bits of the brain are active when
people do different things, and which parts

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.510
of the brain aren’t working in patients.
Some of the really cool things you can do

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.909
with MEG include brain-computer interfaces,
where a computer can monitor and respond to

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:34.769
brain activity. In one study, a computer was
set up to move a cursor on a screen when a

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:39.810
particular kind of brain activity was detected
by MEG. Stoke victims who couldn’t move

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.120
their hands learned to produce that kind of
brain activity – so they were able to move

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.769
the cursor with their minds. When the computer
was connected to a mechanical hand, the stroke

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:53.810
victims could open and close the hand with their
brain activity. Similar stuff has been done

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.629
with monkeys controlling robotic arms. With
this kind of technology, we can potentially

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:03.290
restore movement to people who can’t move,
and communication to people who can’t speak.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.950
We can even work out what people are seeing
and thinking by measuring their brain activity.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.950
Much of this kind of research involves implants
in the brain rather than MEG, but it’s great

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.239
when MEG can be used because it’s so easy
and non-invasive. But yeah the point is that

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:21.770
technologies like MEG are taking us closer
and closer to Luke Skywalker robot-hands and

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:25.230
mind reading.
In summary, magnetoencephalography works by

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:30.530
using super-sensitive magnetism detectors
called SQUIDs to measure and locate the electrical

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:35.400
activity of the brain. You’ve got to do
it in a shielded room, and with special cooling,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.000
but it’s relatively easy to do, and it has
great spatial and temporal resolution.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:49.780
Thanks for watching. Subscribe for more quick, clear explanations and analysis on stuff like Game of Thrones. Cheers.

